Z||000000||Diamond Joe Quimby||10-16-2000||11:19 AM||||Aloha,<BR>I am having problems with an 84.<BR>The car starts and runs fine for about 20 minutes, but then it stalls and won't start for about 5 minutes. The odd thing is that the check engine light doesn't come on during the period in which it won't start. Once the check engine light comes back on, the car starts right up. The ECM diagnostic shows nothing, but the diagnostic won't work at all (no check engine light) during the five minutes it is stalled.<P>I posted this earlier (here and other places), and the common answer was the pick up coil and iginition module. I have replaced both of them, and the problem still exist. However, the problem isn't as bad. It used to only run 10 minutes before it stalled and take 15 or so before it would start again.<P>Open for ideas. Leaning towards the ECM being bad.<P>Thanks for any assistance,<BR>Jim<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||10-17-2000||11:51 AM||||Sounds like maybe the power to the ECM is intermittent(?).  A test light might help you determine what's going on.  <P>Could be a crack in one of the conductors in an ECM board, I suppose.  Sometimes those can "heal" and break with temperature changes. ||||reg||3
Z||000002||william_thorniii||10-17-2000||12:02 PM||||diamond joe,<P>Have you checket your cat for clogging?<P>william||||reg||5
Z||000003||Diamond Joe Quimby||10-17-2000||12:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>diamond joe,<P>Have you checket your cat for clogging?<P>william</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the tip, I haven't considered that yet. However, there seems to be no indication of restricted flow at any point, but I haven't directly checked it. Also, I don't think that  would affect the check engine light as described.||||reg||1
